Ordered VS Sorted
Unordered - no guaranteed iteration sequence
Ordered - Guaranteed iteration sequence
Sorted - ordered by some logical rule


Common uses for List:
An ordered collection each element has an index, can contain duplicate elements.
One of the most common uses is ArrayList<E> , each index has a place and it is a continuous block on memory

Linked list - each element has a pointer to a next element if we want to add an element at the beginning/end its simple
for end we just update the last pointer to a new node that contains the element.

Common uses for Set:
Represents mathematical set, Can't contain duplicate elements, its unordered and unsorted but we can sort and order it.

common use for set is HashSet - uses a hash table to map the elements. We have a hash table which each one of these elements contains
a pointer to a bucket which is a list of elements.

Linked Hash Set - it also keeps a hash table and has near that hash table it has a linked list that keeps the order in some way (usually by
order of insertion)

Tree Set - its implemented as a balanced tree it allows sorting since it keeps on ordering and sorting searching takes log(n)

Common uses for Map:
Its an object that maps keys to values.
Each key can map to at most value. Can be ordered or not, sorted or not.
Does not implement the collection interface
can be ordered or not, sorted or not.

HashMap - uses hash table same principle like hash set but this time each key shows its value once we inserted the key.
TreeMap - same like tree set just with keys and values

